I'll push back slightly.  I think battery power sounds (is) great, BUT it's not perfect of course.
@2:00.  "Powers every home in SF for 6 hours."   Not businesses?  And what happens at 6:01?
@2:30. "It's been operating flawlessly since it opened." Even better than that -- I read the week before it was officially online it paid for itself 20% by handling a dropout.



@4:14 "batteries don't emit any pollution .. [they get] power from clean wind turbine ... absolutely no air pollution created." Windmills + batteries.  Everything wears out, of course, so maybe not air, but not perfectly clean either.  And any pollution created from manufacturing the batteries?   And I've read the concrete that support those windmills produce lots of CO2 by themselves.
And peaker-plants. They wear out as well and don't come online instantly, but they can generate power rain/shine/wind/snow, or NOT.  I suspect you'll ALWAYS need some peaker plants.


Not _at all_ that battery-walls are bad, they do sound great.  But what's the battery/windmill decommissioned equipment look like 20 years from now?  Better, worse, or similar compared to "peak plants"?  And I guess also an occasional power outage in stressful situations when the batteries temporary run out of juice. (That's what we've got now, so similar.)


It seems much better than current, but make sure you don't accidentally sell it as perfect -- that'll highly disappoint many people.

